#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130043} :activity/id #uuid "7d9fbdd4-9840-5d87-ba9d-a1c1a46d9235" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207 :topic/id #uuid "36521b0a-e13c-5296-b2d4-078f2470ad6d" :topic/math-academy-id 1992} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128983 :question/id #uuid "5110af12-1d0a-5f7e-8a3b-626cc437c53e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6097"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16601513-9b74-5cec-9af6-2d99b98e1ab3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442480}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral"} :step/id #uuid "a902970c-e690-5291-9e44-5c7c70076e17" :step/math-academy-id 22582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128984 :question/id #uuid "bd93891e-1f9f-51af-a5c2-4eb5ca1178d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6098"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d237f9fb-b5d2-53e0-bc28-ee50d9e08852" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051063}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442481}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "07c10315-095f-5ca8-ae6c-b3cafc296205" :step/math-academy-id 22583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128985 :question/id #uuid "e51c2aa9-027b-5b23-bcaf-805804a8de9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8f012d0-444a-5035-8a72-a053de312d63" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442482}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing the Order of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "def04952-960b-574f-ad97-fdf9decfb2de" :step/math-academy-id 22584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130374 :tutorial/content "Let's once again consider the integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{ { (x^2+y^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A}}\n$$\n\nover the rectangular domain \n\n$$\nD=[0,1] \\times [0,1] = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSimilar to definite integrals of single-variable functions, this double integral can be defined as the limit of a Riemann sum, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{f\\left( {x,y} \\right)\\, \\mathrm{d}A}} = \\mathop {\\lim }\\limits_{n,\\,\\,m \\to \\infty } \\sum\\limits_{j = 1}^n {\\sum\\limits_{i = 1}^m {f\\big( {x_i^*,y_j^*} \\big)\\,\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j} }\n$$\n\nNotice that in the above summation, we could have instead summed over the $j$'s first and then the $i$'s, giving\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{f\\left( {x,y} \\right)\\, \\mathrm{d}A}} = \\mathop {\\lim }\\limits_{m,\\,\\,n \\to \\infty } \\sum\\limits_{i = 1}^m {\\sum\\limits_{j = 1}^n {f\\big( {x_i^*,y_j^*} \\big)\\,\\Delta y_j \\Delta x_i} },\n$$\n\nand this would result in an iterated integral in the reverse order: \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{f\\left( {x,y} \\right)\\, \\mathrm{d}A}}  \n= \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 f(x,y)\\ \\mathrm{d}  y\\,  \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\n \n\nRegardless of the order in which we carry out the integration, the answer will always be the same!\n\nMoreover, *provided that the region $D$ is rectangular*, we can swap the order of integration. This means that if \n\n$$\nD=[a,b] \\times [c,d] = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\: c \\leq y \\leq d \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{c}^{d}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "21451089-e080-55c2-acf5-60d200c5ee21" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3859 :tutorial/title "The Order of Integration for Double Integrals on a Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "e9f89a44-c08e-51d9-95f7-d73be3535606" :step/math-academy-id 22621 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130381 :tutorial/content "A **repeated integral**, also known as an **iterated integral,** is an integral in which we carry out repeated integration with respect to different variables. An example of a repeated integral is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $x,$ and then with respect to the outer variable $y$. We can write this more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration. First, we integrate with respect to $x,$ *treating $y$ as a constant*. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{y{e}^{xy}}{y}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{xy}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y & ={[{e}^{y}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[e-1]-[1-0] \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y = e-2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab6d3983-ce7d-5e80-9dfc-ae0644ac5030" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b2b10cfb-2130-565a-9f4e-f0f29bef13a2" :step/math-academy-id 23088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130020}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130412 :tutorial/content "Consider the surface $z= f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nR=[0,1] \\times [0,1] = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of our surface and its domain is shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1fdf4668c7e89a9a32ee8e655fe5c80c899a04d25a0f342365560f4d9abdd8fd.png)\n\n \n\nThe **double integral** of $f(x,y)$ over $R,$ denoted\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{R}{ f(x,y) \\, \\mathrm{d}A},\n$$\n\nrepresents the total *signed* volume bounded between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the planes $x=0, x=1, y=0, y=1.$\n\nIt can be shown that the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} {{(3-x^2-y^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A}},\n$$\n\ncan be evaluated using the repeated integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 (3-x^2-y^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}  x\\,  \\mathrm{d}  y.\n$$\n\nNotice that we replace $\\textrm d A$ with $\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y$ when writing our double integral as a repeated integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "b04cc8c6-0b4d-5f4e-9027-892d33558737" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5203 :tutorial/title "Double Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "dfebef0f-d872-5cc5-bcc5-6e2befa67e6b" :step/math-academy-id 32682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130021}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Rectangular Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129338}]}
